{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '250,315p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py\nsed -n '544,556p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py\nsed -n '1,105p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/f18ec8cc.py\nsed -n '250,262p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/f18ec8cc.py\"","aggregated_output":"                    v = int(out[rr, cc])\n                    if v == bg:\n                        continue\n                    adj_counts[v] = adj_counts.get(v, 0) + 1\n        if not adj_counts:\n            continue\n\n        # Prefer an adjacent color that exists in the mapping; pick the most frequent border.\n        border = None\n        best = -1\n        for col, cnt in adj_counts.items():\n            if col not in mapping:\n                continue\n            if cnt > best:\n                best = cnt\n                border = col\n        if border is None:\n            continue\n\n        fill = int(mapping[border])\n        for r, c in pts:\n            out[r, c] = fill\n\n\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Fill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n    - A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n    - Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n    - For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\n    Key insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = _bg_boundary(g)\n\n    mapping, pal_mask = _palette_mapping(g, bg)\n    assert mapping, \"main: palette mapping must be non-empty\"\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    _fill_enclosed_bg_regions(out, bg, mapping, pal_mask)\n\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (c) ---------------\n\n# -- Configuration constants --------------------------------------------------\n# Grid dimensions\n_GRID_H_MIN, _GRID_H_MAX = 4, 13\n_GRID_W_MIN, _GRID_W_MAX = 10, 15\n_SMALL_H_THRESHOLD = 6        # below this, prefer left/right palette\n_SMALL_H_PALETTE_FLIP = 0.7   # probability of flipping top/bottom -> left/right\n_TINY_H_THRESHOLD = 5         # below this, cap sprite height at 3\n\n# Palette\n_PALETTE_N_MIN, _PALETTE_N_MAX = 2, 5\n\n# Sprite geometry\n_SPRITE_H_MAX = 7\n_SPRITE_W_MAX = 6\ndef generate_input():\n    for _ in range(_MAX_RETRIES):\n        r = _gen_attempt()\n        if r is not None:\n            return r\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate input after {_MAX_RETRIES} attempts\")\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport itertools\nimport random\n\n\nBlock = tuple[int, int, int]  # c0, c1_exclusive, dominant color\n\n\ndef _unique_mode(values: np.ndarray) -> int:\n    counts = Counter(map(int, values.reshape(-1)))\n    ranked = counts.most_common()\n    assert ranked, \"expected a nonempty sequence\"\n    assert len(ranked) == 1 or ranked[0][1] > ranked[1][1], \"expected a unique dominant color\"\n    return int(ranked[0][0])\n\n\ndef _vertical_blocks(g: np.ndarray) -> list[Block]:\n    height, width = map(int, g.shape)\n    assert height > 0 and width > 0, \"expected a nonempty grid\"\n\n    column_colors = [_unique_mode(g[:, c]) for c in range(width)]\n    blocks: list[Block] = []\n    start = 0\n    current = column_colors[0]\n    for col, color in enumerate(column_colors[1:], start=1):\n        if color != current:\n            blocks.append((start, col, current))\n            start = col\n            current = color\n    blocks.append((start, width, current))\n\n    assert len(blocks) >= 3, \"expected at least three vertical color blocks\"\n    base_colors = [color for _, _, color in blocks]\n    assert len(set(base_colors)) == len(base_colors), \"block colors must be unique\"\n    return blocks\n\n\ndef _common_marker_rows(g: np.ndarray, blocks: list[Block]) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n    base_colors = {color for _, _, color in blocks}\n    rows: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n    for r in range(g.shape[0]):\n        shared = base_colors.copy()\n        for c0, c1, _ in blocks:\n            shared &= set(map(int, g[r, c0:c1]))\n        if shared:\n            assert len(shared) == 1, \"expected each marker row to name one block color\"\n            rows.append((r, shared.pop()))\n    return rows\n\n\ndef _block_order(g: np.ndarray, blocks: list[Block]) -> list[int]:\n    block_count = len(blocks)\n    base_colors = [color for _, _, color in blocks]\n    marker_rows = _common_marker_rows(g, blocks)\n\n    if len(marker_rows) == block_count:\n        assert len({color for _, color in marker_rows}) == block_count, \"marker rows must name unique blocks\"\n        row_numbers = [row for row, _ in marker_rows]\n        marker_colors = [color for _, color in sorted(marker_rows)]\n        if max(row_numbers) - min(row_numbers) + 1 == block_count and marker_colors == list(reversed(base_colors)):\n            return list(reversed(range(block_count)))\n    else:\n        assert len(marker_rows) in (0, 1), \"incomplete marker rows should be absent or unique\"\n\n    return list(range(1, block_count)) + [0]\n\n\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Reorder the vertical color-major blocks while preserving every block's internal pixels.\n\n    Input structure\n    The grid is divided into vertical blocks, each with a unique dominant color.\n    Small marker pixels from other block colors may appear inside the blocks.\n\n    Rule\n    If adjacent shared-color marker rows read exactly as the current block order\n    from right to left, reverse the blocks. Otherwise move the leftmost block to\n    the far right and shift the remaining blocks left.\n\n    Key insight\n    The transformation acts on whole vertical blocks, so marker pixels move with\n    their source block instead of being recolored or independently rearranged.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    assert g.ndim == 2, \"expected a 2D grid\"\n\n    blocks = _vertical_blocks(g)\n    order = _block_order(g, blocks)\n    assert sorted(order) == list(range(len(blocks))), \"block order must be a permutation\"\n\n    return np.concatenate([g[:, blocks[i][0] : blocks[i][1]] for i in order], axis=1)\n\n# --------------- generator (o5x) ---------------\n# Canvas, block, and color bounds.\nGEN_HEIGHT_RANGE = (7, 9)\nGEN_TOTAL_WIDTH_RANGE = (10, 15)\nGEN_BLOCK_COUNT_CHOICES = [3, 4]\nGEN_BLOCK_WIDTH_RANGE = (3, 7)\nGEN_COLOR_POOL = range(1, 10)\n\n# Marker-layout probabilities and row-sharing limits.\nGEN_REVERSE_PROB = 0.35\ndef generate_input():\n    for _ in range(GEN_MAX_ATTEMPTS):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid f18ec8cc input\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,110p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/d749d46f.py\nsed -n '226,238p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/d749d46f.py\nsed -n '1,115p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/753ea09b.py\nsed -n '564,576p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/753ea09b.py\nsed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed56627177/15/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,110p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/d749d46f.py\nsed -n '226,238p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/d749d46f.py\nsed -n '1,115p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/753ea09b.py\nsed -n '564,576p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/753ea09b.py\nsed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed56627177/15/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"import numpy as np\n\nfrom arc_utils import colors_present, find_components, get_bg\nimport random\n\n\ndef _assert_output_footprints_disjoint(dims: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> None:\n    occupied: set[tuple[int, int]] = set()\n\n    c = 0\n    for h, w in dims:\n        short, long = min(h, w), max(h, w)\n        cells = {(r, cc) for r in range(short) for cc in range(c, c + long)}\n        assert not (occupied & cells), \"normalized output rectangles must not overlap\"\n        occupied |= cells\n        c += long + 1\n\n    c = 0\n    for h, w in dims:\n        short, long = min(h, w), max(h, w)\n        cells = {\n            (r, cc)\n            for r in range(10 - long, 10)\n            for cc in range(c, c + short)\n        }\n        assert not (occupied & cells), \"normalized output rectangles must not overlap\"\n        occupied |= cells\n        c += short + 1\n\n\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Pack each solid foreground rectangle twice: wide across the top and tall from the bottom.\n\n    Input structure\n    The grid has one background color and one foreground color; the foreground forms\n    separate solid rectangles, ordered from left to right.\n\n    Rule\n    Build a 10-row background canvas whose width is the packed width of the wide\n    versions of the rectangles. For each source rectangle, paint a top-aligned\n    rectangle of shape min(h,w) by max(h,w), with one background column between\n    consecutive rectangles. Then, starting again from the left edge, paint a\n    bottom-aligned rectangle of shape max(h,w) by min(h,w) for each source rectangle,\n    again separated by one background column.\n\n    Key insight\n    The input rectangles are not copied in their original orientations; each object\n    contributes one horizontal-facing and one vertical-facing normalized rectangle,\n    preserving left-to-right object order.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = get_bg(g)\n    fg_colors = colors_present(g, exclude={bg})\n    assert len(fg_colors) == 1\n    fg = fg_colors[0]\n\n    components = find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, colors=[fg])\n    assert components\n    components = sorted(components, key=lambda obj: (obj[\"bbox\"][1], obj[\"bbox\"][0]))\n\n    dims: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n    for obj in components:\n        r0, c0, r1, c1 = obj[\"bbox\"]\n        h = r1 - r0 + 1\n        w = c1 - c0 + 1\n        assert obj[\"area\"] == h * w\n        dims.append((h, w))\n\n    out_h = 10\n    out_w = sum(max(h, w) for h, w in dims) + len(dims) - 1\n    _assert_output_footprints_disjoint(dims)\n    out = np.full((out_h, out_w), bg, dtype=int)\n\n    c = 0\n    for h, w in dims:\n        short, long = min(h, w), max(h, w)\n        assert long <= out_h\n        out[:short, c : c + long] = fg\n        c += long + 1\n\n    c = 0\n    for h, w in dims:\n        short, long = min(h, w), max(h, w)\n        assert long <= out_h\n        out[out_h - long : out_h, c : c + short] = fg\n        c += short + 1\n\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (att1) ---------------\n\n# Color palette.\nCOLOR_VALUES = tuple(range(10))\n\n# Rectangle counts and dimensions.\nRECT_COUNT_MIN = 4\nRECT_COUNT_MAX = 6\nSHORT_SIDE_MIN = 1\nSHORT_SIDE_MAX = 3\nSIDE_SUM_MAX = 10\nLONG_SIDE_MAX = SIDE_SUM_MAX - SHORT_SIDE_MIN\n\n# Canvas and packing constraints.\nARC_GRID_MAX = 30\nOUTPUT_HEIGHT = 10\nGAP_WIDTH = 1\n\n# Retry policy.\nMAX_GENERATION_ATTEMPTS = 300\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(MAX_GENERATION_ATTEMPTS):\n        result = _gen_attempt()\n        if result is not None:\n            return result\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {MAX_GENERATION_ATTEMPTS} attempts\")\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nfrom arc_utils import colors_present, get_bg, label_mask, neighbors4, paste\nimport itertools\nimport random\n\n\ndef _unique_largest_color(g: np.ndarray, colors: list[int]) -> int:\n    counts = Counter(int(v) for v in g.ravel())\n    ranked = sorted(colors, key=lambda color: counts[int(color)], reverse=True)\n    assert len(ranked) >= 2, \"expected at least two candidate colors\"\n    assert counts[ranked[0]] > counts[ranked[1]], \"foreground majority must be unique\"\n    return int(ranked[0])\n\n\ndef _long_component_ids(lab: np.ndarray, n: int) -> set[int]:\n    h = int(lab.shape[0])\n    min_row_span = max(1, h // 3)\n    ids: set[int] = set()\n    for comp_id in range(1, n + 1):\n        coords = np.argwhere(lab == comp_id)\n        assert coords.size > 0, \"component labels must be nonempty\"\n        r0 = int(coords[:, 0].min())\n        r1 = int(coords[:, 0].max())\n        if r1 - r0 + 1 >= min_row_span:\n            ids.add(int(comp_id))\n    assert len(ids) >= 2, \"expected at least two long contour components\"\n    return ids\n\n\ndef _two_exterior_bg_ids(bg_lab: np.ndarray, n_bg: int) -> set[int]:\n    assert n_bg >= 3, \"expected interior and exterior background regions\"\n    sizes = {comp_id: int((bg_lab == comp_id).sum()) for comp_id in range(1, n_bg + 1)}\n    size_values = sorted(sizes.values(), reverse=True)\n    assert size_values[1] > size_values[2], \"two exterior regions must be separated by size\"\n    cutoff = size_values[1]\n    exterior = {comp_id for comp_id, size in sizes.items() if size >= cutoff}\n    assert len(exterior) == 2, \"expected exactly two exterior background regions\"\n    return exterior\n\n\ndef _touches_long_contour(\n    coords: np.ndarray,\n    fill_lab: np.ndarray,\n    long_ids: set[int],\n) -> bool:\n    for r, c in coords:\n        for rr, cc in neighbors4(int(r), int(c)):\n            if 0 <= rr < fill_lab.shape[0] and 0 <= cc < fill_lab.shape[1]:\n                if int(fill_lab[rr, cc]) in long_ids:\n                    return True\n    return False\n\n\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Fill the small background compartments bounded by the long contour color.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - The grid has one dominant background color and two foreground colors.\n    - The more frequent foreground color forms long 8-connected contour fragments.\n    - The other foreground color is a fixed distractor/obstacle color that partitions\n      the background but is never recolored.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Split the background into 4-connected regions using all foreground pixels as\n      barriers.\n    - Keep the two largest background regions as the outside of the contour drawing.\n    - Recolor every other background region that touches a long contour component\n      to the contour color; leave all foreground pixels unchanged.\n\n    Key insight:\n    The apparent area fill is exactly a component-level fill of bounded background\n    compartments, not a row-wise interpolation between individual contour pixels.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = get_bg(g)\n    foreground = colors_present(g, exclude={bg})\n    assert len(foreground) == 2, \"expected exactly two foreground colors\"\n\n    fill_color = _unique_largest_color(g, foreground)\n    fill_lab, _n_fill = label_mask(g == fill_color, connectivity=8)\n    long_ids = _long_component_ids(fill_lab, _n_fill)\n\n    bg_lab, n_bg = label_mask(g == bg, connectivity=4)\n    exterior_ids = _two_exterior_bg_ids(bg_lab, n_bg)\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    for comp_id in range(1, n_bg + 1):\n        if comp_id in exterior_ids:\n            continue\n        coords = np.argwhere(bg_lab == comp_id)\n        if _touches_long_contour(coords, fill_lab, long_ids):\n            out[bg_lab == comp_id] = fill_color\n\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (att2) ---------------\n\n# Canvas and palette.\nH, W = 30, 30\nGRID_SHAPE = (H, W)\nARC_COLOR_COUNT = 10\n\n# Long contour path layout.\nPATH_COUNT_OPTIONS = (2, 3)\nPATH_STEP_OPTIONS = (-1, 0, 1)\nPATH_GAP_MIN, PATH_GAP_MAX = 2, 5\nPATH_LEFT_MIN_COL, PATH_RIGHT_MARGIN = 2, 3\nPATH_START_MIN_COL = 3\nPATH_START_RIGHT_MARGIN = 4\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(GENERATION_ATTEMPTS):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate valid 753ea09b input\")\n你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4c7dc4dd\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nLearn a transformation rule from a training pair of framed sprites, then apply it to a test sprite.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid contains several colored square frames (hollow borders of a single color).\n- Each frame's interior holds a small pattern sprite; one frame's interior is blank (all black).\n- Two frames form a training pair (their interiors share the same color palette); one remaining frame is the test case.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the training pair by matching interior color sets; the other non-blank frame is the test input.\n- Infer the rule mapping source→destination (e.g. reflection, color inversion, gap filling, endpoint extraction, or checkerboard complement).\n- Apply that same rule to the test sprite; output the transformed interior.\n\nKey insight: The puzzle is an analogy — \"A is to B as C is to ?\" — where you must discover the visual rule from one example pair and generalize it.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"984d8a3e\" lines=\"5-21\">\n<description>\nShift each row's dominant middle-color run left until its right edge aligns with the leftmost original right edge, moving displaced left-side pixels after the run.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has three colors.\n- The most common color forms one contiguous horizontal run in every row.\n- Pixels before those runs share one side color, and pixels after those runs share the other side color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the minimum right edge column among the middle-color runs.\n- In each row, keep the run width and the trailing right-side color count.\n- Place the middle-color run so its right edge is at that minimum column, unless the run is too wide, in which case start it at column 0.\n- Fill any remaining non-trailing cells with the left-side color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe varying row prefixes are movable padding; the stable feature is the leftmost right boundary of the middle-color shape.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"78e78cff\" lines=\"71-91\">\n<description>\nRecolor the row-wise passage indicated by wall pixels with the singleton marker color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background contains one singleton marker pixel and one wall color.\n- Wall pixels lie on several rows and form left/right limits around the marker column.\n- Empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows are open horizontal passages.\n\nRule:\n- On a wall row, paint background pixels between the nearest wall strictly\n  left of the marker column and the nearest wall at or right of it; if only\n  one side is present, paint from that wall to the opposite grid edge.\n- On empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows, paint the whole row.\n- On empty rows outside the wall-row band, reuse the nearest end wall-row interval.\n- Preserve wall pixels and the original marker pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The wall color defines scanline limits relative to the marker column; the\n  marker color fills those implied horizontal spans rather than flood-filling\n  through connected background.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2601afb7\" lines=\"30-45\">\n<description>\nRotate each vertical bar's color one slot right and its height one slot left.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background surrounds several monochrome one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Every bar is bottom-aligned and occupies a distinct column.\n- The ordered bar slots are read from left to right.\n\nRule\n- Keep the grid shape and the bar columns unchanged.\n- At slot i, use the color from the previous slot cyclically.\n- At slot i, use the height from the next slot cyclically.\n\nKey insight\n- Color and height are independent cyclic attributes of the ordered bar slots:\n  colors rotate right while heights rotate left.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"db0c5428\" lines=\"32-51\">\n<description>\nExpand a 3x3 block motif into a 5x5 block motif around its missing center.\n\nInput structure:\n- Teal background surrounds one 9x9 foreground motif.\n- The motif is a 3x3 array of 3x3 blocks; the middle block is empty teal.\n- Colored pixels immediately around the empty middle block encode the border\n  of the missing center block.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the original 9x9 motif in place.\n- Copy each of the eight surrounding 3x3 blocks to the opposite outer slot\n  of the implied 5x5 block grid, without rotating it.\n- Fill the center block from the eight pixels around the hole; its center is\n  the motif's majority foreground color.\n\nKey insight: The visible 9x9 motif is the middle of a larger periodic\nblock-level pattern, and the empty center is reconstructed from its immediate\ninner boundary.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6ca952ad\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nDrop every large foreground object straight down to the bottom while leaving small marker objects fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Objects of area at most three are stationary markers; larger objects are moving sprites.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each moving sprite from its original position.\n- Translate each moving sprite vertically downward until its bbox bottom reaches the grid bottom.\n- Repaint each translated sprite in its original color; small markers stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Object area separates the active falling sprites from the small fixed markers, and all active sprites follow the same vertical path.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"230f2e48\" lines=\"19-38\">\n<description>\nFold each straight red rod at its black marker, leaving the gray-anchored side fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each foreground object is a straight horizontal or vertical 4-connected rod.\n- Each rod contains many pixels of one line color, one endpoint marker, and one\n  interior control marker.\n\nRule:\n- For every rod, keep the segment from the endpoint marker through the control\n  marker unchanged.\n- Move the line-color tail on the far side of the control marker to a\n  perpendicular ray starting next to that marker.\n- Choose the perpendicular direction with more space to the grid edge.\n\nKey insight:\n- The endpoint marker identifies the fixed side of the rod, while the control\n  marker is the hinge; the tail folds into the larger open half-plane.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3d588dc9\" lines=\"95-109\">\n<description>\nReplace the active black cutout's tail with background and mark the cutout side facing a colored source region.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is background, with black rectangle-plus-tail cutouts, one gray source region, and colored distractors.\n- Non-gray colored regions and single pixels are distractors/blockers, even when they are multi-pixel staircase shapes.\n\nRule:\n- Find each black cutout whose largest solid rectangle leaves exactly a two-pixel tail.\n- The active gray source region is visible only by horizontal rays through background from a cutout's left or right side.\n- Select the unique cutout side with the most same-source visible rows, erase that cutout's tail to background, and recolor the full selected vertical side pink (6).\n\nKey insight:\n- The colored regions do not move; their horizontal line-of-sight identifies which rectangular cutout is being opened and which side receives the pink mark.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5751f35e\" lines=\"29-43\">\n<description>\nComplete sparse nested rectangular color layers into solid rectangles.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color, and every foreground color marks one\naxis-aligned rectangular layer; the layer bboxes have unique areas and are\nnested from outermost to innermost.\nRule\nTake each color's bbox as its completed rectangle, except that a child bbox\nwhich already matches at least three sides of its parent's one-pixel inset\nexpands to that full inset; repaint the rectangles largest to smallest.\nKey insight\nThe scattered pixels are samples of nested rectangular regions, so the\noutput is a clean redraw of those regions with inner colors overwriting\nouter colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"41ace6b5\" lines=\"7-26\">\n<description>\nNormalize vertical two-color bars between paired marker columns around the marker rows.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- A row of red marker pixels sits directly above a row of gray marker pixels\n  in the same alternating columns.\n- The columns between adjacent markers contain vertical bars made from a\n  contiguous teal run above a contiguous blue run.\n\nRule\n- Keep the marker pixels and all non-bar background cells fixed.\n- Let h be the maximum teal-run height among the bar columns; paint teal in\n  every bar column for h rows ending at the red marker row.\n- In each bar column, move its blue run to start at the gray marker row and\n  fill all lower cells in that column with maroon.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker rows are a baseline: teal is normalized into a common upper\n  band, while each blue run keeps its own height below the baseline.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e39e9282\" lines=\"73-81\">\n<description>\nProject maroon markers into adjacent grey or pink 3x3 squares, erasing grey squares afterward.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
